{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792776816,"nonce":"lHAsMLkO4hsOesYAF/byf+Qw/gE=","title":"TypeScript: bounds proofs for module array bindings","message":"Flow facts are never kept for a module global, since any call may reassign it, so element reads of a module array in a function always take the checked index path even inside a canonical loop such as for (let i = 0; i \u003c data.length; i++) data[i]. Loading the global into a local first restores the proofs, because list headers keep identity and only grow. A safe improvement: inside a loop whose body makes no calls and no writes to the binding, treat the global like a local for bounds facts, or load it once into a hidden local at the loop head. Measure before and after; no claims until benchmarking starts. Parent: 751af73.","files":null}]}